    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Food and Nutrition</span> is the scientific study of food and how the body uses it. It combines knowledge from biology, chemistry, and health sciences to understand how nutrients support life, growth, and well-being. Understanding basic terms and factors affecting nutrition is the foundation of this subject.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Basic Definitions</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Food</td><td>Any substance taken into the body that provides nutrients for energy, growth, repair, and regulation of body processes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>The science of food and how the body uses it; the process by which the body takes in and uses nutrients</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nutrient</td><td>A chemical substance in food that the body needs for growth, energy, repair, and regulation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Malnutrition</td><td>A condition resulting from an inadequate, excessive, or imbalanced intake of nutrients</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Undernutrition</td><td>Insufficient intake of nutrients — the body gets less than it needs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Overnutrition</td><td>Excessive intake of nutrients — more than the body needs (leading to obesity, etc.)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Antinutrient</td><td>A substance in food that interferes with the absorption or utilisation of nutrients (e.g., phytates in grains, oxalates in spinach)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pro-vitamin</td><td>A substance that can be converted into a vitamin in the body (e.g., beta-carotene → Vitamin A)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Dietetics</td><td>The application of nutrition science to planning and promoting healthy diets for individuals and groups</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nutritionist</td><td>A professional who studies and applies the science of nutrition</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Importance of Food and Nutrition</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Energy provision:</strong> Food provides the energy (calories) needed for all bodily activities</li>\n  <li><strong>Growth and development:</strong> Nutrients provide building materials for cell and tissue growth</li>\n  <li><strong>Body repair and maintenance:</strong> Nutrients repair worn-out tissues and cells</li>\n  <li><strong>Disease prevention:</strong> Good nutrition strengthens the immune system and prevents deficiency diseases</li>\n  <li><strong>Regulation of body processes:</strong> Vitamins, minerals, and water regulate metabolic processes</li>\n  <li><strong>Mental health and cognitive function:</strong> Adequate nutrition supports brain development and function</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Factors Affecting Food and Nutrition</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Available income</td><td>Higher income → more food variety; low income → limited food choices</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Availability of food</td><td>Food that is not available locally cannot be consumed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Season</td><td>Certain foods available only in specific seasons; price fluctuates</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Culture and religion</td><td>Cultural practices and religious beliefs dictate what foods are eaten or avoided</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Education</td><td>Knowledge of nutrition guides better food choices</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Age and physiological state</td><td>Needs vary by age, pregnancy, lactation, illness</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Storage and processing</td><td>Poor storage and processing reduce nutritional value</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Advertising and media</td><td>Media influences food preferences and choices</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key distinction: MALNUTRITION is the umbrella term covering BOTH undernutrition (too little) AND overnutrition (too much). An ANTINUTRIENT reduces nutrient absorption (e.g., phytates in beans reduce iron and zinc absorption — soaking and cooking reduces phytates). A PRO-VITAMIN is not a vitamin itself but is converted to one (beta-carotene → Vitamin A).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Food = substance providing nutrients. Nutrition = science of food use. Malnutrition = inadequate/excessive/imbalanced nutrient intake. Antinutrient = interferes with nutrient absorption. Pro-vitamin = converted to a vitamin in body. Importance: energy, growth, repair, disease prevention, regulation. Factors: income, availability, season, culture, education, age.\n</div>\n  ",
