    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Food and Nutrition does not exist in isolation — it draws from and connects to many other academic disciplines. Understanding these <span class=\"learn-keyword\">interrelationships</span> helps us appreciate why the study of food and nutrition requires knowledge from multiple fields and how each contributes to our understanding of food, health, and the human body.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Food and Nutrition with Biology</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Anatomy and physiology of the digestive system — how the body digests and absorbs nutrients</li>\n  <li>Cell biology — how nutrients are used at cellular level</li>\n  <li>Biochemistry of nutrient metabolism</li>\n  <li>Microbiology — how microorganisms affect food safety and fermentation</li>\n  <li>Plant biology — understanding food crops, their composition, and nutritional value</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Food and Nutrition with Chemistry</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Composition of macronutrients (carbohydrates, proteins, fats) and micronutrients</li>\n  <li>Chemical reactions during cooking (Maillard reaction, caramelisation, oxidation)</li>\n  <li>Food analysis and testing — identifying nutrients and contaminants</li>\n  <li>Understanding food additives (preservatives, emulsifiers, colourings)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Food and Nutrition with Health Science/Medicine</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Understanding nutritional deficiency diseases and their treatment</li>\n  <li>Diet management in disease prevention and treatment</li>\n  <li>Drug-nutrient interactions</li>\n  <li>Public health nutrition and epidemiology</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Food and Nutrition with Agriculture</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Production of food crops and livestock — sources of nutrients</li>\n  <li>Post-harvest handling and storage affecting nutritional quality</li>\n  <li>Biofortification — developing nutrient-rich crop varieties</li>\n  <li>Food security and sustainable food systems</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Food and Nutrition with Economics/Social Sciences</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Food pricing, availability, and access</li>\n  <li>Impact of poverty and income on nutrition</li>\n  <li>Food policy and government nutrition programmes</li>\n  <li>Marketing and consumer behaviour related to food choices</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Food and Nutrition with Mathematics and Statistics</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Calculating nutrient content of diets and recipes</li>\n  <li>Measuring and weighing ingredients</li>\n  <li>Statistical analysis of nutrition surveys and research</li>\n  <li>Dietary reference values and percentage calculations</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> For each subject relationship, be able to give specific examples. Biology → digestion, cell metabolism. Chemistry → nutrient composition, food testing. Agriculture → food production, biofortification. Economics → food prices, poverty and nutrition. Mathematics → nutrient calculations. Medicine/Health Science → deficiency diseases, therapeutic diets.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Food and Nutrition interrelates with: Biology (digestion, metabolism, microbiology), Chemistry (nutrient composition, food testing, cooking reactions), Health Science/Medicine (deficiency diseases, therapeutic diets), Agriculture (food production, biofortification), Economics (food access, poverty), Mathematics (calculations, measurements, statistics). It is a multidisciplinary science.\n</div>\n  ",
