    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Nigeria has an enormous variety of locally available foods that are nutritious, affordable, and culturally appropriate. Understanding <span class=\"learn-keyword\">local foods</span> and their nutritional qualities enables better dietary planning using available resources, reducing dependence on expensive imported foods.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Classification of Local Foods by Nutrient Function</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Functional Group</th><th>Primary Nutrient</th><th>Nigerian Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Energy/Body-building foods</td><td>Carbohydrates</td><td>Yam, cassava, rice, maize, plantain, breadfruit, sorghum, millet</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Body-building foods</td><td>Proteins</td><td>Beans, cowpea, soybeans, groundnuts, meat, fish, eggs, snails, crayfish</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Protective foods</td><td>Vitamins and minerals</td><td>Ugwu, bitter leaf, tomatoes, peppers, carrots, oranges, mangoes, garden eggs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Energy foods (fats)</td><td>Fats and oils</td><td>Palm oil, groundnut oil, coconut oil, avocado, melon (egusi)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Important Nigerian Local Foods and Their Nutrients</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Food</th><th>Primary Nutrients</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Yam (isu)</td><td>Carbohydrates, some Vitamin C, potassium</td><td>Staple food in southern Nigeria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cassava/Garri</td><td>Carbohydrates (mainly starch)</td><td>Contains cyanogenic glucosides (reduced by fermentation/cooking)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Beans/Cowpea</td><td>Protein, iron, B vitamins</td><td>Excellent plant protein; complementary with cereals</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ugwu (fluted pumpkin leaves)</td><td>Iron, Vitamin A, Vitamin C, calcium, folate</td><td>Very nutritious green leafy vegetable</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Palm oil</td><td>Fat, Vitamin A (beta-carotene), Vitamin E</td><td>Red palm oil rich in carotenoids</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Crayfish</td><td>Protein, calcium, iron</td><td>Used as condiment; highly nutritious in small amounts</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Groundnuts</td><td>Protein, fat, B vitamins, zinc</td><td>Both protein and fat source; groundnut oil widely used</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Garden eggs (eggplant)</td><td>Fibre, Vitamin C, minerals</td><td>Low calorie; antioxidant properties</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Importance of Using Local Foods</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>More affordable and accessible than imported foods</li>\n  <li>Better adapted to the local climate and available year-round or seasonally</li>\n  <li>Familiar in taste and preparation to local populations</li>\n  <li>Support local agriculture and economy</li>\n  <li>Many local foods are highly nutritious (ugwu, palm oil, beans)</li>\n  <li>Reduce food miles and environmental impact</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know Nigerian foods by their functional category: ENERGY (yam, cassava, rice, maize), BODY-BUILDING (beans, meat, fish, eggs, crayfish), PROTECTIVE (ugwu, tomatoes, peppers, fruits). Red PALM OIL is special — it provides fat AND Vitamin A (beta-carotene, the pro-vitamin A that gives it its red colour).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigerian local foods classified by function: energy foods (yam, cassava, rice — carbohydrates), body-building foods (beans, fish, meat, eggs — protein), protective foods (ugwu, tomatoes, fruits — vitamins and minerals), energy foods from fat (palm oil, groundnut oil). Palm oil rich in Vitamin A. Beans complement cereals for complete protein. Using local foods is affordable and nutritionally sound.\n</div>\n  ",
