    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Carbohydrates</span> are the body's primary and preferred source of energy. They are the most abundant macronutrient in the diets of most Nigerians, found in staple foods like yam, cassava, rice, and maize. Understanding their composition, classification, functions, and dietary significance is fundamental to food and nutrition science.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Chemical Composition of Carbohydrates</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Carbohydrates are organic compounds composed of three elements: <strong>Carbon (C), Hydrogen (H), and Oxygen (O)</strong>. Their general formula is: C<sub>n</sub>(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>n</sub> — hence the name 'hydrates of carbon.' The ratio of hydrogen to oxygen is always 2:1 (same as water).</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Examples: Glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆); Sucrose (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁); Starch (C₆H₁₀O₅)n</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Classification of Carbohydrates</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Example</th><th>Food Source</th><th>Property</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Monosaccharides (simple sugars — 1 unit)</td><td>Glucose, Fructose, Galactose</td><td>Fruits, honey, milk</td><td>Simplest form; directly absorbed; sweet</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Disaccharides (2 units)</td><td>Sucrose (glucose+fructose); Maltose (glucose+glucose); Lactose (glucose+galactose)</td><td>Sucrose: sugar cane; Lactose: milk; Maltose: malted barley</td><td>Sweet; broken down to monosaccharides in digestion</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Polysaccharides (many units)</td><td>Starch (digestible); Glycogen (storage); Cellulose (dietary fibre)</td><td>Starch: yam, rice, cassava; Glycogen: liver, muscle; Cellulose: plant cell walls</td><td>Not sweet; large molecules; cellulose not digested by humans</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Functions of Carbohydrates</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Primary energy source:</strong> Glucose provides 4 kcal/g; powers all body activities, especially brain</li>\n  <li><strong>Protein sparing:</strong> Adequate carbohydrate prevents protein being used for energy</li>\n  <li><strong>Fat metabolism:</strong> Carbohydrates needed for complete fat oxidation ('fat burns in the flame of carbohydrates')</li>\n  <li><strong>Dietary fibre (cellulose):</strong> Aids bowel movement, prevents constipation, reduces cholesterol</li>\n  <li><strong>Energy storage:</strong> Excess carbohydrate stored as glycogen (liver and muscle) or converted to fat</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Dietary Deficiency of Carbohydrates</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Fatigue, weakness, and lethargy</li>\n  <li>Hypoglycaemia (low blood sugar) — dizziness, shakiness</li>\n  <li>Ketosis — body breaks down fat and protein for energy, producing ketone bodies</li>\n  <li>Protein catabolism (protein used for energy rather than growth and repair)</li>\n  <li>Weight loss</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Nigerian Food Sources of Carbohydrates</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Yam, cassava (garri, fufu), rice, maize (corn, ogi), millet, sorghum, plantain, breadfruit, sweet potato, cocoyam</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Carbohydrates contain C, H, O (ONLY — no nitrogen). Classification: Monosaccharides (1 unit: glucose, fructose, galactose), Disaccharides (2 units: sucrose, lactose, maltose), Polysaccharides (many units: starch, glycogen, cellulose). CELLULOSE is not digested by humans — it is dietary fibre. GLYCOGEN is the storage form in animal tissues. Energy yield: 4 kcal per gram.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Carbohydrates = C, H, O; formula Cn(H2O)n. Classes: monosaccharides (glucose, fructose, galactose), disaccharides (sucrose, maltose, lactose), polysaccharides (starch, glycogen, cellulose). Functions: primary energy (4 kcal/g), protein-sparing, fat metabolism, fibre (cellulose). Deficiency: fatigue, ketosis, protein catabolism. Nigerian sources: yam, cassava, rice, maize.\n</div>\n  ",
