    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Proteins</span> are the body's building materials — essential for growth, repair, enzyme production, immune function, and many other vital processes. They are the only macronutrients that contain nitrogen, making them unique among the major nutrients. Understanding protein chemistry, quality, and dietary significance is crucial.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Chemical Composition of Proteins</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Proteins are made up of <strong>Carbon (C), Hydrogen (H), Oxygen (O), Nitrogen (N)</strong>. Some proteins also contain Sulphur (S), Phosphorus (P), and Iron (Fe). The presence of NITROGEN (16% by weight) distinguishes proteins from carbohydrates and fats.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The basic building units are <strong>amino acids</strong> — over 20 different amino acids form all body proteins.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Classification of Amino Acids</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Essential amino acids</td><td>Cannot be synthesised by the body — MUST be obtained from diet (9 essential for adults: + histidine)</td><td>Lysine, methionine, tryptophan, leucine, valine, isoleucine, threonine, phenylalanine, histidine</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Non-essential amino acids</td><td>Can be synthesised by the body from other compounds</td><td>Alanine, glutamic acid, aspartic acid, glycine</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Conditionally essential</td><td>Essential under certain conditions (illness, growth, stress)</td><td>Arginine, glutamine, cysteine, tyrosine</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Classification of Proteins by Quality</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Complete (high biological value) proteins:</strong> Contain all essential amino acids in adequate proportions — animal proteins (meat, fish, eggs, milk). Egg protein is the reference standard (biological value of 100).</li>\n  <li><strong>Incomplete (low biological value) proteins:</strong> Lack or are deficient in one or more essential amino acids — most plant proteins (beans are low in methionine; cereals are low in lysine)</li>\n  <li><strong>Limiting amino acid:</strong> The essential amino acid present in lowest relative amount in a food protein</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Complementary and Supplementary Values of Protein</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Complementation:</strong> Combining two incomplete proteins that make up for each other's deficiencies to provide a complete amino acid profile. Example: beans (high lysine, low methionine) + rice (high methionine, low lysine) = complete amino acid profile.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Supplementation:</strong> Adding a small amount of a high-quality (complete) protein to improve the quality of a larger amount of incomplete protein. Example: adding a small amount of crayfish or egg to a predominantly cereal diet.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Functions of Proteins</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Growth, development, and body building</li>\n  <li>Repair and replacement of worn-out tissues</li>\n  <li>Production of enzymes (biological catalysts)</li>\n  <li>Production of hormones (insulin, growth hormone)</li>\n  <li>Antibody production (immune defence)</li>\n  <li>Transport (haemoglobin transports oxygen; albumin transports fatty acids)</li>\n  <li>Energy provision (4 kcal/g — used when carbohydrate and fat are insufficient)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Dietary Deficiency Diseases</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Kwashiorkor:</strong> Severe protein deficiency (with adequate or near-adequate energy) — oedema (fluid retention/swelling), muscle wasting, pot belly, skin changes, hair discolouration, irritability</li>\n  <li><strong>Marasmus:</strong> Severe energy and protein deficiency (starvation) — extreme wasting, no oedema, 'skin and bone' appearance</li>\n  <li><strong>Protein-Energy Malnutrition (PEM):</strong> Spectrum of conditions including kwashiorkor and marasmus</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Proteins contain C, H, O, N (and sometimes S). NITROGEN is the key element that distinguishes proteins from carbohydrates and fats. COMPLETE proteins = animal foods (egg, meat, fish, milk). INCOMPLETE = most plant foods. KWASHIORKOR = protein deficiency + oedema + pot belly. MARASMUS = total starvation (energy + protein) + extreme wasting, no oedema.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Proteins = C, H, O, N (nitrogen is unique). Building blocks = amino acids (9 essential — must come from diet). Complete proteins: animal foods. Incomplete: plant foods. Complementation: beans + rice. Functions: growth, repair, enzymes, hormones, antibodies, transport, energy (4 kcal/g). Deficiency: Kwashiorkor (protein) and Marasmus (energy + protein).\n</div>\n  ",
