    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Fats and oils</span> (collectively called lipids) are concentrated energy sources and essential nutrients. They often cause confusion because people associate them only with weight gain, but they perform many vital functions that no other nutrient can replace. Understanding the differences between fats and oils, their classification, and dietary role is essential.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Differences Between Fats and Oils</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Fats</th><th>Oils</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Physical state at room temperature</td><td>Solid</td><td>Liquid</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Main fatty acid type</td><td>Predominantly saturated fatty acids</td><td>Predominantly unsaturated fatty acids</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Source</td><td>Mainly animal sources</td><td>Mainly plant sources</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Butter, lard, suet, tallow, coconut fat (solid)</td><td>Palm oil, groundnut oil, olive oil, soybean oil</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Melting point</td><td>Higher (solid at room temperature)</td><td>Lower (liquid at room temperature)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Chemical Composition of Fats and Oils</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Like carbohydrates, fats contain only Carbon (C), Hydrogen (H), and Oxygen (O) — but with a much higher proportion of hydrogen and much less oxygen. Chemically, most dietary fats are <strong>triglycerides</strong> — one glycerol molecule + three fatty acid chains.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Classification of Fatty Acids</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Food Sources</th><th>Effect on Health</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Saturated fatty acids (SFA)</td><td>No double bonds; solid at room temperature</td><td>Animal fats, butter, coconut oil, palm kernel oil</td><td>Raise LDL (bad) cholesterol — linked to CVD risk</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Monounsaturated fatty acids (MUFA)</td><td>One double bond; liquid at room temperature</td><td>Olive oil, avocado, groundnut oil</td><td>Neutral or beneficial; lower LDL, maintain HDL</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA)</td><td>Two or more double bonds; liquid at room temperature</td><td>Sunflower oil, fish oils (omega-3), soybean oil</td><td>Beneficial; lower LDL; omega-3 anti-inflammatory</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Trans fatty acids</td><td>Artificially hydrogenated; solid-like</td><td>Margarine, fried fast food, processed snacks</td><td>Most harmful: raise LDL, lower HDL</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Functions of Fats and Oils</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Energy provision:</strong> 9 kcal/g — the most concentrated energy source</li>\n  <li><strong>Fat-soluble vitamin absorption:</strong> Vitamins A, D, E, K require fat for absorption</li>\n  <li><strong>Insulation:</strong> Subcutaneous fat insulates against cold</li>\n  <li><strong>Protection of organs:</strong> Padding fat around kidneys, liver, heart</li>\n  <li><strong>Cell membrane structure:</strong> Phospholipids are essential components</li>\n  <li><strong>Essential fatty acid provision:</strong> Linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) cannot be synthesised</li>\n  <li><strong>Hormone precursors:</strong> Steroid hormones (sex hormones, cortisol) made from cholesterol</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Dietary Deficiency Symptoms</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Deficiency of essential fatty acids: dry scaly skin, poor wound healing, impaired brain development</li>\n  <li>Deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) — all require fat for absorption</li>\n  <li>Loss of body insulation — poor temperature regulation</li>\n  <li>Weight loss and reduced energy</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Fats = SOLID at room temperature (mainly saturated/animal). Oils = LIQUID at room temperature (mainly unsaturated/plant). Fat provides 9 kcal/g — MORE than carbohydrate or protein (both 4 kcal/g). Essential fatty acids = linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) — MUST come from diet. Fat required for fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, K.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Fats = solid (saturated, animal). Oils = liquid (unsaturated, plant). Both = C, H, O (triglycerides = glycerol + 3 fatty acids). Functions: energy (9 kcal/g), fat-soluble vitamin absorption, insulation, organ protection, cell membranes, essential fatty acids. Saturated fats raise LDL. Unsaturated (especially omega-3) beneficial. Trans fats most harmful.\n</div>\n  ",
