    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Vitamins</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">minerals</span> are micronutrients — needed in small amounts but absolutely essential for health. They do not provide energy but regulate and support almost every biological process. Their deficiencies cause specific diseases that are preventable with adequate diet.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Classification of Vitamins</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Vitamins</th><th>Characteristics</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Fat-soluble</td><td>A, D, E, K</td><td>Stored in body fat and liver; not needed daily; risk of toxicity if excess stored</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Water-soluble</td><td>B complex (B1, B2, B3, B5, B6, B7, B9, B12) and C</td><td>Not stored (except B12); needed daily; excess excreted in urine; less risk of toxicity</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Key Vitamins — Functions, Deficiency, Sources</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Vitamin</th><th>Chemical Name</th><th>Key Function</th><th>Deficiency Disease</th><th>Nigerian Food Source</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>A</td><td>Retinol / beta-carotene (pro)</td><td>Vision (especially night), growth, skin, immune function</td><td>Night blindness, xerophthalmia</td><td>Palm oil, ugwu, carrots, liver, eggs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>B1</td><td>Thiamine</td><td>Carbohydrate metabolism (coenzyme)</td><td>Beriberi (nerve damage, heart failure)</td><td>Whole grains, beans, pork</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>B2</td><td>Riboflavin</td><td>Energy metabolism, growth</td><td>Ariboflavinosis (cracked lips, sore tongue)</td><td>Liver, milk, eggs, leafy vegetables</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>B3</td><td>Niacin</td><td>Energy metabolism (NAD coenzyme)</td><td>Pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia — '3 Ds')</td><td>Meat, fish, groundnuts</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>B9</td><td>Folic acid/Folate</td><td>DNA synthesis, red blood cell formation; neural tube development</td><td>Megaloblastic anaemia; neural tube defects in pregnancy</td><td>Ugwu, beans, liver, dark green vegetables</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>B12</td><td>Cobalamin</td><td>Red blood cell formation, nerve function, DNA synthesis</td><td>Pernicious anaemia, nerve damage</td><td>Meat, fish, eggs, milk (absent in plant foods)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>C</td><td>Ascorbic acid</td><td>Antioxidant, collagen synthesis, iron absorption</td><td>Scurvy (bleeding gums, poor wound healing)</td><td>Oranges, tomatoes, peppers, guava</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>D</td><td>Calciferol</td><td>Calcium and phosphorus absorption; bone mineralisation</td><td>Rickets (children), osteomalacia (adults)</td><td>Sunlight (skin synthesis), fish liver oils, eggs</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Key Minerals — Classification and Functions</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Mineral</th><th>Key Function</th><th>Deficiency</th><th>Nigerian Sources</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Calcium (Ca)</td><td>Bone and teeth formation, muscle contraction, blood clotting</td><td>Osteoporosis, rickets, tetany</td><td>Milk, crayfish, ugwu, bones</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Iron (Fe)</td><td>Part of haemoglobin (oxygen transport)</td><td>Iron-deficiency anaemia (pallor, fatigue)</td><td>Liver, ugwu, beans, red meat</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Iodine (I)</td><td>Thyroid hormone synthesis (regulates metabolism)</td><td>Goitre, cretinism, hypothyroidism</td><td>Seafood, iodised salt</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Zinc (Zn)</td><td>Immune function, wound healing, growth, taste/smell</td><td>Poor wound healing, growth retardation, impaired immunity</td><td>Meat, beans, nuts, crayfish</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sodium (Na)</td><td>Fluid balance, nerve impulses</td><td>Hyponatraemia; excess causes hypertension</td><td>Salt, cured foods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Phosphorus (P)</td><td>Bone, teeth, ATP (energy), DNA</td><td>Rare (widespread in foods)</td><td>Meat, fish, dairy, beans</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> VITAMINS: Fat-soluble = A, D, E, K (stored in body). Water-soluble = B vitamins + C (not stored, needed daily). Key diseases: Night blindness = Vit A; Beriberi = Vit B1; Pellagra = Vit B3 (3Ds: Dermatitis, Diarrhoea, Dementia); Scurvy = Vit C; Rickets = Vit D. MINERALS: Anaemia = Iron deficiency; Goitre = Iodine deficiency; Osteoporosis = Calcium deficiency.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Vitamins: fat-soluble (A, D, E, K — stored) and water-soluble (B, C — not stored). Key deficiencies: A → night blindness; B1 → beriberi; B3 → pellagra; C → scurvy; D → rickets. Minerals: Ca (bones, teeth), Fe (haemoglobin/anaemia), I (thyroid/goitre), Zn (immunity, wound healing). All micronutrients regulate body processes without providing energy.\n</div>\n  ",
