    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Water</span> is the most essential nutrient — life cannot exist without it. The human body is approximately 60-70% water by weight. Unlike food, survival without water is measured in days (2-4 days without water vs weeks without food). Water performs vital functions that no other substance can replace.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Chemical Composition of Water</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Water is composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom: <strong>H₂O</strong>. It is a polar molecule — the unequal distribution of charge makes it an excellent solvent for polar and ionic substances. It has a high specific heat capacity and high heat of vaporisation.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Functions of Water in the Body</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Universal solvent:</strong> Dissolves nutrients, metabolic products, and waste products for transport</li>\n  <li><strong>Transport medium:</strong> Blood (mainly water) transports nutrients, oxygen, hormones, and waste products</li>\n  <li><strong>Temperature regulation:</strong> Evaporation of sweat cools the body; high heat capacity buffers temperature changes</li>\n  <li><strong>Lubricant:</strong> Lubricates joints (synovial fluid), eyes (tears), digestive tract</li>\n  <li><strong>Metabolic reactions:</strong> Participates as a reactant in hydrolysis reactions (digestion) and is a product of oxidation reactions (metabolic water)</li>\n  <li><strong>Medium for chemical reactions:</strong> All metabolic reactions occur in aqueous solution</li>\n  <li><strong>Structural function:</strong> Maintains cell shape and turgor; forms part of blood, lymph, and interstitial fluid</li>\n  <li><strong>Cushioning:</strong> Cerebrospinal fluid (around brain and spinal cord), amniotic fluid (around foetus)</li>\n  <li><strong>Waste removal:</strong> Dissolved in urine for kidney excretion; in perspiration</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Sources of Water</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Beverages</td><td>Drinking water, juices, milk, tea, soups</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Food (high water content)</td><td>Fruits (watermelon 92%, oranges 87%), vegetables (cucumbers 96%), milk (88%), meat (75%), eggs (75%)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Metabolic water</td><td>Produced internally when carbohydrates, fats, and proteins are oxidised for energy</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Water Requirements and Dehydration</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Adults need approximately 2–3 litres per day (from all sources)</li>\n  <li><strong>Dehydration</strong> symptoms: thirst, concentrated urine, headache, dizziness, dry mouth, fatigue, reduced physical and cognitive performance</li>\n  <li>Severe dehydration: confusion, organ failure, death</li>\n  <li>Dehydration risk: high temperatures, vigorous exercise, illness (diarrhoea, fever)</li>\n  <li>Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS) replaces water and electrolytes lost during diarrhoea</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Water = H₂O (2 hydrogen + 1 oxygen). The human body is 60-70% water. Water does NOT provide energy (0 kcal). Functions: solvent, transport, temperature regulation, lubricant, metabolic reactions. Dehydration is a medical emergency — survival without water = only 2-4 days. Metabolic water is produced when nutrients are oxidised.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Water = H₂O; body is 60-70% water. Functions: universal solvent, transport medium, temperature regulation (sweating), lubricant, metabolic reactions (hydrolysis), structural. Sources: drinking water, beverages, food, metabolic water. Dehydration: thirst, headache, reduced cognition → severe → organ failure. Adults need 2-3 litres/day.\n</div>\n  ",
