    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The scientific study of foods requires precise <span class=\"learn-keyword\">measurement</span> and accurate techniques. Whether weighing ingredients, measuring portions, or conducting food analysis, accuracy is fundamental to producing reliable results in food science, nutrition research, and practical cookery.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Measurements in Food Science</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Food science uses both metric (SI) and household measurement systems:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Metric Unit</th><th>Common Household Equivalents</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Mass/Weight</td><td>kilogram (kg), gram (g)</td><td>1 kg = 1000 g; 1 tablespoon flour ≈ 15g</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Volume</td><td>litre (L), millilitre (mL)</td><td>1 L = 1000 mL; 1 cup ≈ 240 mL; 1 tbsp ≈ 15 mL</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Temperature</td><td>degrees Celsius (°C)</td><td>100°C = boiling point; 180°C = moderate oven</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Energy</td><td>kilocalorie (kcal), kilojoule (kJ)</td><td>1 kcal = 4.184 kJ</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Food Weighing</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Accurate weighing of food is essential for:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Dietary assessment — recording how much of each food is consumed</li>\n  <li>Recipe standardisation — ensuring consistent results</li>\n  <li>Calculating nutrient content using food composition tables</li>\n  <li>Portion control in therapeutic and institutional catering</li>\n  <li>Food labelling — determining nutritional information per serving</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types of Scales and Weighing Instruments</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Use</th><th>Feature</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Platform/kitchen scale</td><td>Weighing food portions in cooking</td><td>Capacity 1–5 kg; accuracy ±1–5 g</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Electronic/digital scale</td><td>Precise food weighing in research and clinical settings</td><td>High accuracy ±0.1–1 g; tare function</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Beam balance (laboratory)</td><td>Research and analytical weighing</td><td>Very accurate; requires calibration</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bathroom/body weight scale</td><td>Weighing individuals for anthropometric assessment</td><td>Capacity up to 150–200 kg</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Handy (Household) Measures</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">When weighing equipment is not available, handy measures provide useful approximations:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Teaspoon (tsp):</strong> ≈ 5 mL; used for salt, spices, oil</li>\n  <li><strong>Tablespoon (tbsp):</strong> ≈ 15 mL; used for sugar, flour, fat</li>\n  <li><strong>Cup:</strong> ≈ 240 mL; used for flour, liquid, rice</li>\n  <li><strong>Palm/handful:</strong> approximate measure for nuts, seeds, vegetables</li>\n  <li><strong>Fist:</strong> approximately one cup volume; useful for portion guidance</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Accuracy and Precision in Food Measurement</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> How close the measurement is to the true value</li>\n  <li><strong>Precision:</strong> How reproducible the measurement is (same result repeatedly)</li>\n  <li><strong>Sources of error:</strong> Incorrect calibration, parallax error, wet/damp weighing containers, using wrong instruments</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Metric system: kg/g for mass; L/mL for volume; °C for temperature; kcal/kJ for energy. Key conversions: 1 kg = 1000 g; 1 L = 1000 mL; 1 kcal = 4.184 kJ; 1 tbsp = 15 mL; 1 cup = 240 mL. The TARE function on scales allows you to subtract the weight of the container. Accuracy = correct; Precision = reproducible (consistent).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Food science measurement: metric units (kg, g, L, mL, °C, kcal). Food weighing needed for: dietary assessment, recipe standardisation, nutrient calculation, portion control. Scales: platform (kitchen), digital (research), beam balance (laboratory). Handy measures: tsp (5 mL), tbsp (15 mL), cup (240 mL). Accuracy = correctness; precision = reproducibility.\n</div>\n  ",
