    "topicCode": "SS1-FN-11",
    "module": "Food Science",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Cooking changes the nutritional content of food. Some of these changes improve digestion and nutrient availability, while others destroy or reduce valuable nutrients. Understanding how <span class=\"learn-keyword\">heat affects nutrients</span> helps in choosing cooking methods that preserve maximum nutritional value.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Effects of Heat on Carbohydrates</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Gelatinisation of starch:</strong> When starch granules absorb water and are heated (60–80°C), they swell, burst, and form a thick gel — making starch more digestible. Essential for cooking yam, rice, beans, and porridge.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dextrinisation:</strong> Dry heating of starch (baking, toasting) partially breaks it down into shorter chains called dextrins — producing the brown colour and flavour of toasted bread.</li>\n  <li><strong>Caramelisation:</strong> When sugars are heated above 160°C without water, they break down and form brown compounds and flavourful products (caramel).</li>\n  <li><strong>Maillard reaction:</strong> Non-enzymatic browning between reducing sugars and amino acids — produces brown colour and complex flavours in baked and fried foods.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Effects of Heat on Proteins</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Denaturation:</strong> Heat unfolds and changes the three-dimensional structure of proteins — e.g., egg white turning solid when cooked. Usually begins at 60–70°C.</li>\n  <li><strong>Improved digestibility:</strong> Denaturation makes protein more accessible to digestive enzymes — cooking generally increases protein digestibility.</li>\n  <li><strong>Coagulation:</strong> Proteins coagulate (become solid) when heated — e.g., eggs, meat firming up.</li>\n  <li><strong>Destruction of enzyme inhibitors:</strong> Trypsin inhibitors in beans (antinutrients that block protein digestion) are destroyed by cooking — improving protein digestibility significantly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Maillard reaction:</strong> Can reduce availability of lysine (essential amino acid) at very high temperatures — overheating reduces protein nutritional value.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Effects of Heat on Fats and Oils</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Melting:</strong> Solid fats (butter, lard) melt when heated — reversible on cooling.</li>\n  <li><strong>Smoking point:</strong> Each oil has a temperature above which it begins to smoke — producing harmful compounds (acrolein). Exceeding the smoking point destroys fat-soluble vitamins and produces toxic compounds.</li>\n  <li><strong>Oxidation (rancidity):</strong> Repeated heating oxidises unsaturated fatty acids — producing rancid flavours and harmful oxidation products.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hydrolysis:</strong> Water + heat can hydrolyse triglycerides into fatty acids and glycerol (particularly in deep frying).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Effects of Heat on Vitamins</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Vitamin</th><th>Heat Stability</th><th>Effect of Cooking</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Vitamin C (ascorbic acid)</td><td>Very heat-sensitive</td><td>Easily destroyed by cooking, especially in water; also destroyed by oxygen and light</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>B vitamins (thiamine B1, folic acid B9)</td><td>Heat-sensitive; water-soluble</td><td>Lost into cooking water; destroyed by prolonged cooking and high temperatures</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vitamin A (fat-soluble)</td><td>Moderately stable</td><td>Fairly stable to cooking; some loss at very high temperatures</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vitamin D, E, K (fat-soluble)</td><td>Relatively stable</td><td>Little loss in normal cooking; excessive heat can cause some losses</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Effects of Heat on Minerals</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Minerals themselves do not decompose with heat (they are inorganic elements)</li>\n  <li>However, minerals dissolve into cooking water — vegetables boiled and water discarded lose significant minerals</li>\n  <li>Steaming and microwave cooking preserve more minerals than boiling</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Most HEAT-SENSITIVE nutrients: Vitamin C (most sensitive), Thiamine (B1), Folic acid (B9). HEAT-STABLE: fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), minerals (don't decompose). Protein denaturation IMPROVES digestibility. Trypsin inhibitors in beans are DESTROYED by cooking. Starch GELATINISES with heat + water (improves digestibility).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Heat on carbohydrates: starch gelatinises, caramelisation of sugars, Maillard reaction. Heat on proteins: denaturation, coagulation, improved digestibility, destroys antinutrients. Heat on fats: melting, smoking, oxidation. Heat on vitamins: Vitamin C most heat-sensitive; fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) relatively stable. Minerals dissolve into water but do not decompose with heat.\n</div>\n  ",
