    "topic": "Tests for Proteins",
    "topicCode": "SS1-FN-12",
    "module": "Food Analysis",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Food analysis tests allow us to identify the presence and quantity of specific nutrients in foods. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Tests for proteins</span> use chemical reactions that are specific to amino acids and proteins — producing distinctive colour changes or physical changes that confirm the presence of protein.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Foam Test (for Proteins — particularly saponins/detergent-like proteins)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Principle</td><td>Some proteins (especially those with amphipathic properties) reduce surface tension when agitated, producing persistent foam</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Add water to food sample; shake vigorously for 30 seconds</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Positive result</td><td>Persistent froth/foam indicates the presence of protein (especially saponins or surface-active proteins)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Note</td><td>Not highly specific — other substances (e.g., saponins) also foam</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Millon's Test (for proteins containing tyrosine)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Reagent</td><td>Millon's reagent (mercuric sulphate in nitric acid + sulphuric acid)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Add Millon's reagent to food sample; heat gently</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Positive result</td><td>Brick-red precipitate indicates the presence of protein (specifically tyrosine-containing proteins)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Note</td><td>Specific for phenol groups — tyrosine (an amino acid) reacts with mercuric ions to form red complex</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Biuret Test (for proteins with peptide bonds)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Reagent</td><td>Biuret reagent (dilute copper sulphate CuSO₄ in sodium hydroxide NaOH)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Add sodium hydroxide (NaOH) to sample, then add drops of dilute copper sulphate (CuSO₄)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Positive result</td><td>Purple/violet colour indicates the presence of peptide bonds (proteins and polypeptides)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Negative result</td><td>Remains blue (colour of CuSO₄ alone)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Note</td><td>Most commonly used protein test in food analysis; named after compound 'biuret' (not the reagent colour)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Coagulation Test (for proteins)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Principle</td><td>Proteins coagulate (solidify/precipitate) when heated or treated with acids/alcohol</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Heat the food sample OR add acid (e.g., acetic acid) or alcohol to the sample</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Positive result</td><td>Formation of a white, solid precipitate (coagulum) indicates protein is present</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Example</td><td>Egg white solidifying when heated; milk forming curds when acid is added</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Most important protein test: BIURET TEST — Purple/violet colour with NaOH + CuSO₄ = peptide bonds present = protein. MILLON'S TEST — Brick-red colour with Millon's reagent = tyrosine (protein). COAGULATION — White solid forms when protein heated or acidified. All these tests show PRESENCE (positive) or ABSENCE (negative) of protein.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Protein tests: Foam test (persistent foam = protein/saponins), Millon's test (brick-red with Millon's reagent = tyrosine-containing protein), Biuret test (purple/violet with NaOH + CuSO₄ = peptide bonds/protein — most important), Coagulation (white precipitate with heat or acid = protein). Biuret test = most commonly used in food analysis.\n</div>\n  ",
