    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Just as there are specific tests for proteins, food science uses specific chemical tests to identify <span class=\"learn-keyword\">carbohydrates</span> (starch and reducing sugars) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">fats and oils</span>. These qualitative tests use chemical reactions that are specific to the structural features of each nutrient.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Iodine Test (for Starch)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Reagent</td><td>Iodine solution (iodine dissolved in potassium iodide solution)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Add a few drops of iodine solution to the food sample</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Positive result</td><td>Blue-black colour indicates the presence of starch</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Negative result</td><td>Remains yellow-brown (iodine's own colour)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Principle</td><td>Iodine molecules fit inside the helical structure of amylose (starch) forming a coloured complex</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Benedict's Test / Fehling's Test (for Reducing Sugars)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Reagent</td><td>Benedict's solution (copper sulphate + sodium carbonate + sodium citrate) or Fehling's solution</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Add Benedict's reagent to food sample; heat in boiling water bath for 5 minutes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Positive result</td><td>Colour change from blue → green → yellow → orange → brick-red precipitate (increasing concentration)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Negative result</td><td>Remains blue</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Note</td><td>Detects reducing sugars (glucose, fructose, maltose, lactose) — NOT sucrose (non-reducing)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Litmus Paper Test (for Acidity/Alkalinity in Foods)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Red litmus: turns blue in alkaline conditions</li>\n  <li>Blue litmus: turns red in acidic conditions</li>\n  <li>Useful for testing the pH of acidic foods (vinegar, citrus) and alkaline foods/substances</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Blotting Paper / Grease Spot Test (for Fats and Oils)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Press or rub food sample onto brown paper/blotting paper; hold up to light</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Positive result</td><td>Persistent translucent (greasy) spot on the paper indicates fat/oil present</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Note</td><td>Water also makes paper translucent but dries and disappears; fat stain is permanent</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Sudan III / Sudan IV Test (for Fats and Oils)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Reagent</td><td>Sudan III (orange dye) or Sudan IV (red dye) dissolved in alcohol</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Add Sudan III/IV solution to food sample; observe</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Positive result</td><td>Fat droplets stain orange-red (Sudan III) or scarlet-red (Sudan IV) as Sudan dyes are fat-soluble</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Note</td><td>Useful for detecting fat in histological sections and food samples under microscope</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Ethanol Emulsification Test (for Fats and Oils)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Add food sample to ethanol; shake to dissolve fat; pour into water</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Positive result</td><td>Milky white emulsion forms indicating fat is present (fat droplets remain suspended in water)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Note</td><td>Fat dissolves in ethanol but not water; adding to water creates tiny fat droplets (emulsion)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> STARCH = Iodine test (blue-black positive). REDUCING SUGARS = Benedict's/Fehling's (brick-red positive). FATS = Blotting paper (grease spot), Sudan III (orange-red), Ethanol emulsification (milky white). LITMUS = for acid/alkaline (blue turns red in acid; red turns blue in alkali). All are QUALITATIVE tests.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Tests: Iodine (starch → blue-black), Litmus (acidity/alkalinity), Blotting paper (fat → grease spot), Sudan III (fat → orange-red stain), Ethanol emulsification (fat → milky white). Benedict's/Fehling's (reducing sugars → brick-red). All qualitative tests identify presence of nutrient.\n</div>\n  ",
