    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">digestive system</span> is the organ system responsible for breaking down food into absorbable nutrients and eliminating waste. Understanding digestion, absorption, and metabolism explains how nutrients from food become available to the body's cells.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Basic Metabolic Processes</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Process</th><th>Definition</th><th>Where it occurs</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Digestion</td><td>The mechanical and chemical breakdown of food into smaller absorbable molecules</td><td>Mouth, stomach, small intestine</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Absorption</td><td>The movement of digested nutrients from the digestive tract into the bloodstream or lymphatic system</td><td>Mainly small intestine (ileum); some in colon</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Utilisation (metabolism)</td><td>The use of absorbed nutrients by body cells for energy, growth, repair, and regulation</td><td>All body cells</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Excretion</td><td>The removal of metabolic waste products (urea, CO₂, water) from the body</td><td>Kidneys (urea in urine), lungs (CO₂), skin (sweat)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Enzymes in Digestion</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Enzymes are biological catalysts (proteins) that speed up digestive chemical reactions without being used up. Each enzyme is specific — it catalyses only one type of reaction.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Enzyme</th><th>Location</th><th>Substrate</th><th>Product</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Salivary amylase (ptyalin)</td><td>Mouth (saliva)</td><td>Starch</td><td>Maltose</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pepsin</td><td>Stomach</td><td>Proteins</td><td>Polypeptides</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pancreatic amylase</td><td>Small intestine (pancreatic juice)</td><td>Starch</td><td>Maltose</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Lipase</td><td>Small intestine (pancreatic juice)</td><td>Fats</td><td>Fatty acids + glycerol</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Trypsin/chymotrypsin</td><td>Small intestine (pancreatic juice)</td><td>Proteins/polypeptides</td><td>Shorter peptides</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Peptidases (erepsin)</td><td>Small intestine (intestinal juice)</td><td>Dipeptides</td><td>Amino acids</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Maltase/sucrase/lactase</td><td>Small intestine</td><td>Maltose/sucrose/lactose</td><td>Monosaccharides</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Organs of the Digestive System</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Mouth:</strong> Mechanical digestion (chewing) + salivary amylase (starch digestion begins)</li>\n  <li><strong>Oesophagus:</strong> Transports food from mouth to stomach (peristalsis)</li>\n  <li><strong>Stomach:</strong> Churns food (mechanical); pepsin digests protein; HCl creates acidic pH, kills bacteria</li>\n  <li><strong>Small intestine:</strong> Main site of digestion and absorption; three regions: duodenum, jejunum, ileum</li>\n  <li><strong>Large intestine/Colon:</strong> Water and mineral absorption; faeces formation; gut bacteria</li>\n  <li><strong>Liver:</strong> Produces bile (for fat emulsification); processes absorbed nutrients</li>\n  <li><strong>Pancreas:</strong> Produces digestive enzymes (amylase, lipase, proteases) and bicarbonate</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key definitions: Digestion = mechanical + chemical breakdown. Absorption = movement from gut into blood. Utilisation = use by body cells. Main site of both digestion and absorption = SMALL INTESTINE (ileum). ENZYMES: Amylase (starch → maltose), Pepsin (protein → polypeptides) in stomach, Lipase (fat → fatty acids + glycerol).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Digestive processes: digestion, absorption, utilisation, excretion. Enzymes: salivary amylase (mouth), pepsin (stomach), pancreatic amylase/lipase/proteases (small intestine), disaccharidases (small intestine). Main digestion + absorption in small intestine. Liver: bile. Pancreas: enzymes. Colon: water absorption.\n</div>\n  ",
