    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic traces the complete journey of food from the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">mouth</span> through the digestive system to the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">ileum</span>, describing the specific digestion and absorption events at each stage. Understanding this process explains how food is converted into the nutrients that nourish every cell.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Digestion in the Mouth</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Mechanical:</strong> Chewing by teeth (incisors cut; canines tear; premolars and molars grind) reduces food to small pieces</li>\n  <li><strong>Chemical:</strong> Salivary amylase begins starch digestion: Starch → Maltose</li>\n  <li>Saliva moistens food, forming a bolus for swallowing</li>\n  <li>Tongue shapes the bolus and pushes it to the pharynx for swallowing</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Digestion in the Stomach</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Mechanical:</strong> Churning mixes food with gastric juice; churning breaks food particles further</li>\n  <li><strong>Chemical:</strong> Gastric juice contains:\n    <ul>\n      <li>HCl (hydrochloric acid) — pH 1.5-2; activates pepsinogen; kills bacteria; denatures proteins</li>\n      <li>Pepsin (from pepsinogen activated by HCl) — begins protein digestion: Proteins → Polypeptides</li>\n      <li>Rennin (in infants) — coagulates milk protein (casein) for digestion</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>Products: semi-liquid acidic chyme</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Digestion in the Duodenum (and Small Intestine)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Chyme enters the duodenum from the stomach. The pancreas and liver add secretions:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Pancreatic juice:</strong> Contains sodium bicarbonate (neutralises acid chyme, raising pH to 7-8) + enzymes:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Pancreatic amylase: starch → maltose</li>\n      <li>Lipase (with bile): fat → fatty acids + glycerol</li>\n      <li>Trypsin + chymotrypsin: polypeptides → shorter peptides</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Bile (from liver/gallbladder):</strong> Emulsifies fats — breaks fat globules into tiny droplets for lipase</li>\n  <li><strong>Intestinal juice (succus entericus):</strong> Contains maltase, sucrase, lactase, peptidases (erepsin)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Absorption in the Ileum</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The ileum is the main site of nutrient absorption. Its inner wall has folds, villi, and microvilli (brush border) that dramatically increase surface area:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Nutrient</th><th>Absorbed As</th><th>Route</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Carbohydrates</td><td>Monosaccharides (glucose, fructose, galactose)</td><td>Blood (portal vein → liver)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Proteins</td><td>Amino acids (and small peptides)</td><td>Blood (portal vein → liver)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fats</td><td>Fatty acids + glycerol → reformed as chylomicrons</td><td>Lymph (lacteals → thoracic duct → blood)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K)</td><td>With fat (chylomicrons)</td><td>Lymph → blood</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Water-soluble vitamins (C, B)</td><td>Dissolved in water</td><td>Blood (portal vein)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Minerals</td><td>As ions</td><td>Blood</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Water</td><td>Osmosis</td><td>Blood and lymph</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Mouth → Oesophagus → Stomach (pepsin, HCl) → Duodenum (bile, pancreatic juice) → Ileum (absorption). KEY: FATS are absorbed into LYMPH (lacteals), not blood directly. Glucose and amino acids → blood (portal vein). Fats → lymph → eventually blood (via thoracic duct). The small intestine = MOST IMPORTANT site of digestion + absorption.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Mouth: starch → maltose (salivary amylase). Stomach: proteins → polypeptides (pepsin + HCl). Duodenum: bile emulsifies fat; pancreatic enzymes continue all digestion; intestinal enzymes complete digestion. Ileum: absorbs monosaccharides + amino acids → blood; fatty acids/fat-soluble vitamins → lymph. Large intestine: water absorption, faeces formation.\n</div>\n  ",
