    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Nutrition during reproduction</span> — pregnancy, lactation, and adolescence — has profound effects on the health of both mother and child. Nutritional needs change significantly during these stages, and deficiencies can have lasting consequences for child development and maternal health.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Nutrition During Pregnancy</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Pregnancy increases nutritional demands significantly. Key nutrients needed in greater amounts:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Nutrient</th><th>Why increased</th><th>Deficiency consequence</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Folic acid (B9)</td><td>Neural tube formation; DNA synthesis; rapid cell division</td><td>Neural tube defects (spina bifida, anencephaly)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Iron</td><td>Expanding maternal blood volume; foetal iron stores</td><td>Maternal anaemia; preterm birth; low birth weight</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Calcium</td><td>Foetal bone and teeth development</td><td>Maternal bone loss; poor foetal bone development</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Protein</td><td>Foetal tissue growth; placenta development</td><td>Poor foetal growth; low birth weight</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vitamin A</td><td>Foetal organ development; maternal immunity</td><td>Night blindness; impaired foetal development</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Iodine</td><td>Foetal brain and thyroid development</td><td>Cretinism (severe intellectual disability in baby)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Energy (extra kcal)</td><td>Foetal growth + increased maternal metabolism</td><td>Low birth weight; preterm delivery</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Nutrition During Lactation (Breastfeeding)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Breastfeeding mothers need additional energy (+500 kcal/day), protein, calcium, iron, vitamins A and C</li>\n  <li><strong>Breast milk</strong> is nutritionally complete for infants for the first 6 months (WHO recommendation: exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months)</li>\n  <li>Poor maternal nutrition reduces milk quantity but changes in composition are smaller — the body prioritises infant nutrition at the expense of the mother's stores</li>\n  <li>Fluid intake must increase to support milk production</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Nutrition During Adolescence</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Adolescence is the period of rapid growth (growth spurt) — second only to infancy in growth rate</li>\n  <li>Increased needs: energy, protein, iron (especially girls — menstruation begins), calcium (peak bone mass), zinc (growth and sexual maturation)</li>\n  <li>Adolescent girls: particularly vulnerable to iron deficiency (menstruation) and calcium deficiency (peak bone building period)</li>\n  <li>Disordered eating, peer pressure, and skipping meals are major nutritional risks in adolescence</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Harmful Traditional Practices</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Food taboos for pregnant women (avoiding eggs, meat, some vegetables — reducing protein and vitamin intake)</li>\n  <li>Early marriage and pregnancy before nutritional maturity is reached</li>\n  <li>Inadequate antenatal care and supplementation in pregnancy</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Most critical pregnancy nutrients: FOLIC ACID (neural tube — must start BEFORE conception), IRON (maternal anaemia + foetal stores), CALCIUM (foetal bones), IODINE (foetal brain — cretinism if deficient). Exclusive breastfeeding = 6 months (WHO). Adolescent girls need extra IRON (menstruation) and CALCIUM (peak bone mass building).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Pregnancy: increased folic acid (neural tube), iron, calcium, protein, iodine, energy. Lactation: extra +500 kcal, protein, calcium, vitamins A and C; exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months. Adolescence: rapid growth → more energy, protein, iron (girls), calcium. Food taboos in pregnancy are harmful. Folic acid must be taken before conception for neural tube protection.\n</div>\n  ",
