    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Maintaining a clean kitchen requires appropriate <span class=\"learn-keyword\">cleaning agents</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">abrasives</span>. Using the right cleaning product for each surface and task ensures effective cleaning without damaging equipment or leaving harmful chemical residues on food-contact surfaces.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Classification of Cleaning Agents</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Uses</th><th>Properties</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Water</td><td>Clean water</td><td>General rinsing and washing</td><td>Universal solvent; dissolves water-soluble dirt</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Soap and detergents</td><td>Washing-up liquid, dishwashing soap, bar soap</td><td>Removing grease and food residue from dishes and surfaces</td><td>Surfactants that emulsify grease; must be rinsed off food surfaces</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Disinfectants and sanitisers</td><td>Bleach (sodium hypochlorite), Dettol, Zoflora, quaternary ammonium compounds</td><td>Killing bacteria on surfaces; sanitising food-contact surfaces</td><td>Antimicrobial; toxic if not rinsed properly; not for food-contact surfaces unless food-safe</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Acids</td><td>Vinegar, lemon juice, descalers</td><td>Removing lime scale, mineral deposits, rust stains</td><td>Weak acids dissolve alkaline deposits; natural and commercial forms</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Alkalis</td><td>Baking soda (sodium bicarbonate), washing soda, oven cleaner</td><td>Removing grease, fats, burnt food</td><td>Saponify (emulsify) fats; some are caustic</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Abrasives</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Abrasives use mechanical scrubbing action to remove stubborn dirt and stains:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Abrasive</th><th>Description</th><th>Suitable Surfaces</th><th>Not Suitable</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Steel wool/scouring pads</td><td>Coarse metal mesh pads</td><td>Tough pots (cast iron, stainless steel)</td><td>Non-stick surfaces, aluminium, silver</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Scouring powder</td><td>Powdered abrasive (e.g., Vim)</td><td>Ceramic sinks, stainless steel</td><td>Non-stick surfaces, polished surfaces</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nylon scrubbing pads</td><td>Synthetic non-scratch pads</td><td>Non-stick pans, glass, ceramic</td><td>Not for very tough burnt deposits</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Natural abrasives</td><td>Sand, ash, coconut husk</td><td>Traditional Nigerian pot cleaning</td><td>Modern coated cookware</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Principles of Effective Cleaning</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Pre-soak stubborn dirt to soften it before scrubbing</li>\n  <li>Use the right cleaner for each surface — avoid harsh abrasives on delicate surfaces</li>\n  <li>Always rinse food-contact surfaces thoroughly to remove cleaning agent residues</li>\n  <li>Follow manufacturer's instructions for strong chemicals</li>\n  <li>Clean from high surfaces to low (top-to-bottom) to prevent re-contamination</li>\n  <li>Store cleaning products safely, away from food</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Cleaning agents: water (rinsing), soap/detergent (grease removal), disinfectant/bleach (killing bacteria), acid (lime scale), alkali (grease/fat/burnt food). Abrasives: steel wool (hard pots), nylon pads (non-stick pans). Never use steel wool on non-stick surfaces — damages the coating. Always RINSE food-contact surfaces after cleaning to remove chemical residues.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Cleaning agents: water (universal solvent), soap/detergent (surfactants, emulsify grease), disinfectants/bleach (antimicrobial), acids (lime scale removal), alkalis (fat/grease removal). Abrasives: steel wool (tough pots), scouring powder (sinks), nylon pads (non-stick). Always rinse food-contact surfaces. Store chemicals away from food.\n</div>\n  ",
