    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The kitchen is one of the most hazardous rooms in any home or institution. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Kitchen safety</span> requires constant vigilance — being aware of potential hazards (fire, burns, cuts, slips, chemical exposure, electrical shock) and practising safe habits to prevent accidents.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Common Kitchen Hazards</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Hazard Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Prevention</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Fire and burns</td><td>Overheated oil, gas leaks, unattended cooking</td><td>Never leave cooking unattended; keep flammable materials away from heat</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cuts</td><td>Sharp knives, broken glassware, can lids</td><td>Use sharp knives safely; store properly; dispose of broken glass carefully</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Falls and slips</td><td>Wet floors, spills, cluttered kitchen</td><td>Mop spills immediately; non-slip flooring; keep kitchen clear</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Electrical hazards</td><td>Wet hands near switches, frayed cords, overloaded sockets</td><td>Dry hands before using appliances; check cables regularly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Poisoning</td><td>Cleaning products near food, mislabelled containers</td><td>Store chemicals separately; label all containers clearly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Suffocation and gas</td><td>Gas leaks, smoke, carbon monoxide</td><td>Adequate ventilation; check gas connections; install CO alarm</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Safe Habits in the Kitchen</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Always wear appropriate protective clothing (apron, oven gloves for hot items, closed shoes)</li>\n  <li>Keep hair tied back when cooking</li>\n  <li>Turn pot handles away from the edge of the stove</li>\n  <li>Never leave cooking food unattended</li>\n  <li>Use dry oven gloves/mitts — wet gloves conduct heat</li>\n  <li>Place hot items on heat-resistant surfaces only</li>\n  <li>Never overload electrical sockets</li>\n  <li>Keep children away from the cooking area</li>\n  <li>Know the location of the fire extinguisher/fire blanket</li>\n  <li>Keep a first aid kit accessible in the kitchen</li>\n  <li>Clean up spills immediately to prevent slipping</li>\n  <li>Wash hands before and after handling food</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Fire Safety in the Kitchen</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Never put water on a fat/oil fire (causes explosive splattering) — use a fire blanket or dry powder extinguisher</li>\n  <li>Keep a fire extinguisher and fire blanket accessible</li>\n  <li>Check gas connections regularly</li>\n  <li>If gas leak suspected: do not light flames or switches; open windows; leave and call emergency services</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> NEVER put WATER on a fat fire — water vaporises instantly under burning oil, causing a dangerous explosion of burning droplets. Use FIRE BLANKET (smothers fire) or dry powder extinguisher. Turn pot handles AWAY from the stove edge (prevents tipping). Always use DRY oven gloves (wet gloves conduct heat and burn). Clean up spills IMMEDIATELY (prevents slipping).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Kitchen hazards: fire/burns, cuts, slips, electrical, poisoning, gas. Safe habits: protective clothing, hair tied, pot handles inward, never leave cooking unattended, dry oven gloves, no overloaded sockets. Fire safety: never water on fat fires (use fire blanket/dry powder). Clean spills immediately. Know first aid kit and extinguisher location.\n</div>\n  ",
