    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Hygiene</span> — personal, food, and kitchen hygiene — is the foundation of food safety. Poor hygiene is the leading cause of foodborne illness, which kills millions of people globally each year. Good hygiene practices break the chain of food contamination at multiple points.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Personal Hygiene for Food Handlers</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Handwashing:</strong> Most important hygiene practice — wash hands with soap and water:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Before and after handling food</li>\n      <li>After using the toilet</li>\n      <li>After coughing, sneezing, or blowing nose</li>\n      <li>After handling raw meat, poultry, or fish</li>\n      <li>After touching the face, hair, or phone</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>Bathe regularly; wear clean clothing when handling food</li>\n  <li>Keep nails short and clean (long nails harbour bacteria)</li>\n  <li>Cover cuts and wounds with waterproof dressings</li>\n  <li>Do not sneeze or cough over food</li>\n  <li>Avoid handling food when ill with diarrhoea, vomiting, or skin infections</li>\n  <li>Tie hair back; avoid wearing jewellery that can contaminate food</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Food Hygiene Practices</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Buy food from reputable, clean sources</li>\n  <li>Check expiry dates and food quality before purchasing</li>\n  <li>Store food at correct temperatures (refrigerate perishables at 0-4°C)</li>\n  <li>Keep raw and cooked food separate (prevent cross-contamination)</li>\n  <li>Cook food to correct internal temperatures (kill pathogens)</li>\n  <li>Wash fruits and vegetables thoroughly under running water</li>\n  <li>Use safe (clean) water for food preparation and cooking</li>\n  <li>Avoid the 'temperature danger zone' (5-60°C) — bacteria multiply rapidly</li>\n  <li>Do not thaw frozen food at room temperature (thaw in refrigerator)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Kitchen Hygiene Practices</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Clean and disinfect kitchen surfaces regularly</li>\n  <li>Wash chopping boards and utensils after each use, especially after raw meat</li>\n  <li>Use separate chopping boards for raw meat and vegetables</li>\n  <li>Regularly clean and defrost refrigerator</li>\n  <li>Keep garbage bins covered and empty regularly</li>\n  <li>Pest control — prevent insects and rodents from entering the kitchen</li>\n  <li>Keep dish cloths and sponges clean and dry (breeding ground for bacteria)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Most important hygiene practice = HANDWASHING (with soap and water). Food danger zone = 5-60°C (bacteria multiply rapidly — refrigerate or cook to go outside this range). Never cough/sneeze over food. Separate raw from cooked food. Keep ill food handlers away from cooking. Wash fruits and vegetables under RUNNING water.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Personal hygiene: handwashing (most important), clean clothing, short nails, cover wounds, no illness while cooking. Food hygiene: correct storage temp (0-4°C), separate raw/cooked, cook to safe temp, wash produce, safe water. Kitchen hygiene: clean surfaces and utensils, separate boards, covered bins, pest control. Danger zone: 5-60°C.\n</div>\n  ",
