    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Proper <span class=\"learn-keyword\">waste and refuse disposal</span> in the kitchen and home is a critical public health practice. Improper waste management creates environments for disease-causing pests and organisms, pollutes the environment, and contributes to the spread of foodborne diseases. Sustainable waste management also helps protect the environment.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Kitchen and Food Waste</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Disposal Method</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Organic/biodegradable</td><td>Vegetable peelings, fruit rinds, cooked food scraps, eggshells</td><td>Composting; organic waste bins; animal feed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Recyclable</td><td>Clean tins, glass bottles, plastic containers, paper</td><td>Segregate for recycling; rinse before recycling</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Non-recyclable/general</td><td>Contaminated packaging, foil, mixed material</td><td>General waste bin; responsible landfill disposal</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Hazardous</td><td>Expired medications, cleaning chemicals, batteries</td><td>Special hazardous waste disposal; do NOT put in general waste</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Liquid waste</td><td>Cooking oil, grey water (washing water)</td><td>Used cooking oil: collection points for recycling; not down the drain (blocks sewers)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Principles of Proper Waste Disposal</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Use covered bins with liners — prevents pests and odours</li>\n  <li>Empty bins regularly before overflow (daily for food waste)</li>\n  <li>Keep outdoor garbage areas clean</li>\n  <li>Practice waste segregation (organic, recyclable, general, hazardous)</li>\n  <li>Compost food scraps where possible (reduces waste, improves garden soil)</li>\n  <li>Do not pour used cooking oil down the drain (causes sewer blockages and water pollution)</li>\n  <li>Do not burn waste near the kitchen or where people breathe the smoke</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Public Health Consequences of Improper Waste Disposal</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Provides breeding and feeding sites for disease vectors: flies, mosquitoes, cockroaches, rats</li>\n  <li>Flies carry pathogens from waste to food → foodborne illness</li>\n  <li>Standing water in poorly managed waste → mosquito breeding → malaria, dengue</li>\n  <li>Rat infestation spreads leptospirosis, salmonellosis</li>\n  <li>Environmental pollution — soil, water, and air pollution from improper disposal</li>\n  <li>Aesthetic degradation of the community</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Composting Food Waste</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Composting converts organic kitchen waste into nutrient-rich fertiliser for gardens:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Suitable: vegetable peelings, fruit scraps, eggshells, teabags, coffee grounds</li>\n  <li>Not suitable: cooked meat/fish (attracts pests), dairy, oily foods</li>\n  <li>Benefits: reduces landfill waste, produces free garden fertiliser, reduces greenhouse gas emissions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Waste types: organic (compost), recyclable (clean tins, glass, plastic), general (landfill), hazardous (special disposal). Do NOT pour cooking oil down drains (blocks sewers). Improper waste = flies + rats + cockroaches → spread disease. Composting reduces landfill waste and produces fertiliser. Empty kitchen bins DAILY (food waste attracts pests rapidly).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Kitchen waste types: organic (compost), recyclable, general, hazardous, liquid. Principles: covered bins, empty daily, segregate, compost organic waste, no oil down drain. Public health: improper waste → flies, rats, cockroaches → disease vectors → foodborne illness, malaria. Composting: vegetable peelings, fruit scraps, eggshells → fertiliser.\n</div>\n  ",
