    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Foodborne diseases</span> (food poisoning) are illnesses caused by consuming contaminated food or water. They are a major global public health problem — the WHO estimates that 600 million people suffer foodborne illness annually, with 420,000 deaths. Understanding their causes, symptoms, prevention, and treatment is essential for food safety.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Classification of Foodborne Illness</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Cause</th><th>How it occurs</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Food infection</td><td>Live pathogenic microorganisms consumed with food (bacteria, viruses, parasites)</td><td>Organism grows in the intestine and causes illness</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Food intoxication</td><td>Toxins (poisons) produced by bacteria in food before consumption</td><td>Toxin causes illness — organism may be dead but toxin remains</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Major Foodborne Diseases</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Disease</th><th>Causative Agent</th><th>Common Food Source</th><th>Key Symptoms</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Salmonellosis</td><td>Salmonella bacteria</td><td>Poultry, eggs, meat, dairy (unpasteurised)</td><td>Diarrhoea, vomiting, abdominal cramps, fever (6-72 hrs)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Staphylococcal food poisoning</td><td>Staphylococcus aureus toxin</td><td>Cooked meats, egg/potato salads left at room temperature</td><td>Rapid onset (1-6 hrs): severe vomiting, cramps — no fever</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cholera</td><td>Vibrio cholerae</td><td>Contaminated water, raw shellfish</td><td>Profuse 'rice water' diarrhoea, vomiting, rapid dehydration</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Typhoid fever</td><td>Salmonella typhi</td><td>Contaminated water and food from infected handlers</td><td>High sustained fever, headache, rash, abdominal pain</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>E. coli gastroenteritis</td><td>Escherichia coli (pathogenic strains)</td><td>Contaminated water, undercooked beef, raw vegetables</td><td>Bloody diarrhoea, cramps; O157:H7 can cause kidney failure</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Botulism</td><td>Botulinum toxin (Clostridium botulinum)</td><td>Improperly canned food (anaerobic conditions)</td><td>Neurological: double vision, difficulty swallowing, paralysis</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Prevention of Foodborne Diseases</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>WHO 5 Keys to Safer Food: Keep clean; Separate raw and cooked; Cook thoroughly; Keep food at safe temperatures; Use safe water and raw materials</li>\n  <li>Proper handwashing, food storage, and cooking temperatures</li>\n  <li>Avoiding the temperature danger zone (5-60°C)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Treatment of Foodborne Illness</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Rehydration with ORS (Oral Rehydration Solution) — most important for diarrhoeal illness</li>\n  <li>Rest and supportive care</li>\n  <li>Medical attention for severe cases (bloody diarrhoea, high fever, signs of dehydration)</li>\n  <li>Antibiotics for specific bacterial infections (e.g., typhoid, cholera) — only as prescribed</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Food INFECTION = live organisms consumed (Salmonella, Vibrio cholerae). Food INTOXICATION = pre-formed toxins consumed (Staphylococcus aureus — rapid onset 1-6 hrs; Botulism — neurological). Cholera = 'rice water' diarrhoea, rapid dehydration. Typhoid = sustained high fever. Most common treatment = ORS (rehydration). WHO 5 Keys: Keep Clean, Separate, Cook, Safe Temp, Safe Water.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Foodborne illness types: infection (live organisms) vs intoxication (pre-formed toxins). Key diseases: Salmonella (poultry/eggs), Staphylococcus (cooked food left warm — rapid vomiting), cholera (contaminated water — rice water diarrhoea), typhoid (sustained fever). Botulism: canned food, neurological. Prevention: WHO 5 Keys. Treatment: ORS for dehydration, medical care for severe cases.\n</div>\n  ",
