    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">First aid</span> is the immediate, initial assistance given to a person who has suffered an injury or sudden illness before professional medical care arrives. In the kitchen, common injuries include burns, scalds, cuts, and choking. Knowing first aid can prevent minor injuries from becoming major ones and can save lives in emergencies.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. First Aid Box Content</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A well-stocked first aid box (kit) should contain:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Adhesive bandages/plasters (assorted sizes) — for small cuts and wounds</li>\n  <li>Sterile gauze pads and bandages — for larger wounds</li>\n  <li>Antiseptic solution (e.g., savlon, dettol, hydrogen peroxide) — for cleaning wounds</li>\n  <li>Scissors and tweezers — for cutting bandages and removing splinters</li>\n  <li>Disposable gloves — to protect first aider from contamination</li>\n  <li>First aid instruction booklet</li>\n  <li>Burn dressings — for treating minor burns</li>\n  <li>Thermometer — for checking temperature</li>\n  <li>Eye wash solution and eye bath — for eye injuries</li>\n  <li>Safety pins — for securing bandages</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. First Aid for Burns and Scalds</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Cool the burn immediately with cool (not cold/icy) running water for at least 10 minutes</li>\n  <li>Remove jewellery or clothing near the burn (before swelling occurs)</li>\n  <li>Do NOT apply butter, oil, toothpaste, or any other household remedies (increase infection risk)</li>\n  <li>Cover loosely with a sterile, non-adhesive dressing or cling film</li>\n  <li>Seek medical help for: large burns (>1% body surface area), deep burns, burns on face/hands/genitals</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. First Aid for Cuts and Bleeding</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Apply direct pressure to the wound with a clean cloth or gauze</li>\n  <li>Elevate the injured area above heart level if possible</li>\n  <li>Clean the wound with clean water or antiseptic after bleeding stops</li>\n  <li>Cover with sterile dressing and bandage</li>\n  <li>Seek medical help if: bleeding does not stop in 10 minutes; cut is deep; wound is gaping</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. First Aid for Choking</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>If the person can cough — encourage them to continue coughing</li>\n  <li>If unable to cough: give up to 5 firm back blows between the shoulder blades</li>\n  <li>If back blows fail: up to 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre)</li>\n  <li>Alternate back blows and abdominal thrusts until object is dislodged</li>\n  <li>Call emergency services if the person loses consciousness</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. First Aid for Poisoning</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Call emergency services immediately</li>\n  <li>Do NOT induce vomiting unless specifically instructed</li>\n  <li>Note the substance consumed (label, bottle) to tell medical staff</li>\n  <li>If chemical on skin — flush with large amounts of water</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> BURNS: Cool with running water for 10 MINUTES (not ice, not butter). CUTS: Direct pressure to stop bleeding. CHOKING: Back blows first, then abdominal thrusts (Heimlich). First aid box must include: plasters, gauze, antiseptic, scissors, gloves. NEVER apply butter/toothpaste to burns. Do NOT induce vomiting for chemical poisoning.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> First aid box: plasters, gauze, antiseptic, scissors, gloves, burn dressings. Burns: cool 10+ minutes running water, no butter/toothpaste, sterile cover. Cuts: direct pressure, elevate, clean, cover. Choking: encourage coughing → back blows → abdominal thrusts. Poisoning: call emergency, don't induce vomiting, note substance. Always seek medical help for serious injuries.\n</div>\n  ",
